# Who must approve a change before it can merge (P15-TSK-017, C8; OPERATIONS_RUNBOOK.md section 11.36; THREAT_MODEL.md T11, T28).
#
# UNDER PULL-BASED GITOPS A MERGE IS A DEPLOY. Argo CD syncs production from master, so every path below reaches production, or governs
# what does, the moment it merges: the overlays and everything they render (deploy/, the delivery's Applications and the smoke's
# records included), the infrastructure code (infra/terraform: production's root and the modules it calls), the checks that refuse a
# bad promotion (infra/scripts), the configuration register (every FINANCIAL default changes there in the same pull request as the
# code, ConfigurationRegisterIsCompleteTest), the released-schema floor (moving it is what lets a contract past the lint,
# docs/migrations), and the workflow and this file. CodeOwnersTest holds that each of those resolves to an owner here.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO. It names who must review; GitHub enforces it only once branch protection on master requires a review
# from a code owner, with stale approvals dismissed, the latest push approved by someone other than its author, and no bypass. Those
# are repository settings, the owner's to apply (THREAT_MODEL.md section 6 item 4; the rules are written out in OPERATIONS_RUNBOOK.md
# section 11.36). An author's own approval never counts, so while the repository has one owner a change to any path below cannot merge
# without an administrator's bypass, which those rules forbid: a second person must be named here before production exists.
#
# Last match wins, so a later line naming nobody would un-own a path: none does, and CodeOwnersTest refuses one.
/deploy/                                      @genadigeno
/infra/                                       @genadigeno
/docs/architecture/CONFIGURATION_REGISTER.md  @genadigeno
/docs/migrations/                             @genadigeno
/.github/                                     @genadigeno
